                subtitle: "Baal, Asherah, Molech, Astarte, Dagon",
                emoji: "⛰️",
                sections: [
                    AwakeningSection(heading: "Why Study False Gods?", body: "Here's the uncomfortable truth: you can't recognize a false god if you've never studied what false gods look like.\n\nThe Bible is, among many things, a record of humanity's tendency to worship the wrong things. Not because people are stupid — but because false gods exploit real needs. Baal promised rain for crops. Astarte promised love and fertility. Molech promised protection through sacrifice. Every false god offered something genuinely desired through a counterfeit path.\n\nThat pattern hasn't changed. The names have.\n\nThis module maps false gods in iceberg layers — from the famous ones everyone's heard of, to obscure regional deities, to the deepest level: the pattern itself, which is still running in 2026.\n\nBiblical monotheism doesn't deny other spiritual entities exist. It asserts that one source is supreme and all others are either subordinate or counterfeit (Exodus 20:3; Deuteronomy 32:17). The question isn't whether competing claims on your attention exist. It's whether you can identify them."),
                    AwakeningSection(heading: "Baal: Lord of Storm and Fertility", body: "BAAL (\"Lord\" or \"Master\")\nCanaanite/Phoenician storm and fertility god. Controlled rain, agriculture, reproduction.\n\nWhy people worshiped him: In an agricultural society, no rain = death. Baal promised the one thing everyone needed most — provision. His rituals were tied to crop cycles. Worship him, crops grow. Ignore him, famine.\n\nBiblical confrontation: The ultimate showdown is 1 Kings 18 — Elijah vs. 450 prophets of Baal on Mount Carmel. Baal's prophets cry out all day. Nothing. Elijah prays once. Fire from heaven. The point: the real source doesn't need 450 voices screaming to respond.\n\nArchaeological evidence: Ugaritic texts from Ras Shamra (14th-12th century BCE) depict Baal battling chaos gods. Statues show him with lightning bolts or as a bull (virility symbol). Carthaginian altars confirm rituals including potential child sacrifices.\n\nThe pattern: Baal offered material provision in exchange for spiritual allegiance. That's the oldest trade in human history. And it's still the most common.\n\nASHERAH: THE SACRED FEMININE HIJACKED\nCanaanite fertility goddess, consort of El or Baal. Represented by sacred groves or wooden poles. Associated with trees, the sea, motherhood.\n\nIsrael was explicitly told to destroy Asherah poles (Exodus 34:13). Kuntillet Ajrud graffiti (8th century BCE) reads \"Yahweh and his Asherah\" — some Israelites tried to syncretize. The prophets called this spiritual adultery.\n\nThe pattern: Taking something real (feminine divine energy, the sacred in nature) and building a competing worship system around it."),
                    AwakeningSection(heading: "Molech, Astarte & Dagon", body: "MOLECH: THE PRICE OF PROTECTION\nAmmonite god of fire. Infamous for child sacrifice — \"passing children through the fire\" (Leviticus 18:21; 20:2-5). Heated bronze statues where infants were placed.\n\nWhy people did it: Desperation. When the Assyrians are at the gate and your regular gods aren't answering, you escalate to the most extreme offering you can make. Molech demanded the ultimate sacrifice — your children — in exchange for survival.\n\nThe Valley of Hinnom (Gehenna) where these sacrifices occurred became the Hebrew word for hell. The place where you sacrifice your children to false promises became synonymous with the worst destination imaginable.\n\nArchaeology: Carthaginian tophets (sacrifice sites) with child urns confirm the practice wasn't just biblical polemic.\n\nThe pattern: Sacrificing what's most precious (innocence, future, family) to a false promise of security.\n\nASTARTE (ASHTORETH / ISHTAR)\nPhoenician/Canaanite goddess of love, war, and fertility. Venus star. Solomon's foreign wives introduced her worship (1 Kings 11:5,33).\n\nThe pattern: Seductive power — the promise that desire itself is divine, that pursuing pleasure is worship.\n\nDAGON: NATIONAL PRIDE AS GOD\nPhilistine grain/fertility god. The Ark of the Covenant toppled his statue (1 Samuel 5:1-7). Samson destroyed his temple.\n\nThe pattern: National identity fused with deity — \"our god is our nation, our nation is our god.\" Sound familiar?"),
                ],
